	doi = {10.1007/BF02101898},
	abstract = {We construct the quantum versions of the monodromy matrices of KdV theory. The traces of these quantum monodromy matrices, which will be called as ``\$\{{\textbackslash}bf T\}\$-operators'', act in highest weight Virasoro modules. The \$\{{\textbackslash}bf T\}\$-operators depend on the spectral parameter \$λ\$ and their expansion around \$λ= {\textbackslash}infty\$ generates an infinite set of commuting Hamiltonians of the quantum KdV system. The \$\{{\textbackslash}bf T\}\$-operators can be viewed as the continuous field theory versions of the commuting transfer-matrices of integrable lattice theory. In particular, we show that for the values \$c=1-3\{\{(2n+1){\textasciicircum}2\}{\textbackslash}over \{2n+3\}\} , n=1,2,3,... \$of the Virasoro central charge the eigenvalues of the \$\{{\textbackslash}bf T\}\$-operators satisfy a closed system of functional equations sufficient for determining the spectrum. For the ground-state eigenvalue these functional equations are equivalent to those of massless Thermodynamic Bethe Ansatz for the minimal conformal field theory \$\{{\textbackslash}cal M\}\_\{2,2n+3\}\$; in general they provide a way to generalize the technique of Thermodynamic Bethe Ansatz to the excited states. We discuss a generalization of our approach to the cases of massive field theories obtained by perturbing these Conformal Field Theories with the operator \$Φ\_\{1,3\}\$. The relation of these \$\{{\textbackslash}bf T\}\$-operators to the boundary states is also briefly described.},
