	doi = {10.1016/0550-3213(93)90541-V},
	abstract = {Affine Toda theories with imaginary couplings associate with any simple Lie algebra \$\{{\textbackslash}bf g\}\$ generalisations of Sine Gordon theory which are likewise integrable and possess soliton solutions. The solitons are {\textbackslash}lq{\textbackslash}lq created" by exponentials of quantities \${\textbackslash}hat F{\textasciicircum}i(z)\$ which lie in the untwisted affine Kac-Moody algebra \$\{{\textbackslash}bf{\textbackslash}hat g\}\$ and ad-diagonalise the principal Heisenberg subalgebra. When \$\{{\textbackslash}bf g\}\$ is simply-laced and highest weight irreducible representations at level one are considered, \${\textbackslash}hat F{\textasciicircum}i(z)\$ can be expressed as a vertex operator whose square vanishes. This nilpotency property is extended to all highest weight representations of all affine untwisted Kac-Moody algebras in the sense that the highest non vanishing power becomes proportional to the level. As a consequence, the exponential series mentioned terminates and the soliton solutions have a relatively simple algebraic expression whose properties can be studied in a general way. This means that various physical properties of the soliton solutions can be directly related to the algebraic structure. For example, a classical version of Dorey's fusing rule follows from the operator product expansion of two \${\textbackslash}hat F\$'s, at least when \$\{{\textbackslash}bf g\}\$ is simply laced. This adds to the list of resemblances of the solitons with respect to the particles which are the quantum excitations of the fields.},
